  <h2>What the Text Editor Can Do</h2>

  <p>
    The editor is deliberately lean, but covers everything you need for everyday writing:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Multiple documents in tabs:</strong> work on several texts in parallel and switch with one click.</li>
    <li><strong>Formatting:</strong> font, size, line height, bold, italic, underline, strikethrough, highlight, links, colors.</li>
    <li><strong>Paragraphs and lists:</strong> heading styles, quotes, bullet and numbered lists, alignment left, center, right or justified.</li>
    <li><strong>Insert images:</strong> place photos or graphics directly in the text.</li>
    <li><strong>Page layout:</strong> A4 in portrait or landscape, zoom and word wrap — the text looks like the printed page while you write.</li>
    <li><strong>Markdown &amp; preview:</strong> view the text as Markdown or check the formatted preview.</li>
    <li><strong>Focus mode:</strong> hide all controls and write without distraction.</li>
    <li><strong>Statistics:</strong> words, characters (with and without spaces), lines, sentences, paragraphs and estimated reading time — live in the status bar.</li>
    <li><strong>Find, undo, redo, print, save:</strong> the standard functions you know from a desktop editor.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step by Step: Writing and Exporting Text</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Open the Text Editor</strong>
        <p>Go to <a href="https://kodinitools.com/texteditor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kodinitools.com/texteditor</a> — free, no registration.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Create a new document or open a file</strong>
        <p>"New" starts an empty document, "Open" loads an existing text file from your computer. Each document gets its own tab.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Write and format</strong>
        <p>Pick font, size and style in the toolbar. Lists, quotes and alignment are one click away. Use "Image" to insert graphics at the cursor position.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Review</strong>
        <p>The status bar shows word count and reading time. "Preview" shows the finished layout, "Find" locates terms in the text.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="step-content">
        <strong>Save or print</strong>
        <p>"Save" downloads the text as a file. "Print" opens the browser's print dialog — from there you can also save the document as a PDF.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <p><strong>Tip for bloggers and developers:</strong> use the Markdown view to write text you can paste straight into CMS systems, GitHub READMEs or note-taking apps — without leftover formatting from Word.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>When Is an Online Text Editor the Right Choice?</h2>

  <p>
    Not every text needs a full office program. A browser editor is the better choice when you:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>work on someone else's computer or a tablet where software cannot be installed,</li>
    <li>need to capture a draft, meeting minutes or a note quickly,</li>
    <li>have to check the word count for an article, a job application or an essay,</li>
    <li>want to print a cleanly formatted text or pass it on as a PDF.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Privacy: Your Text Stays With You</h2>

  <p>
    The text editor processes everything locally in the browser. No text and no file is sent to a server — neither while writing nor when saving. That makes the tool suitable for confidential content such as job applications, contracts or internal notes.
  </p>

